LICENSE — DECISION PENDING

Najm OS is intended to be open source, but no license has been finalized yet.

This matters more than it might seem for a project like this: if you intend to
eventually build a commercial product or service on top of this kernel (e.g.
paid support, a Vault Realm app store, hosted tooling), a permissive license
like MIT or Apache-2.0 allows anyone — including a well-funded competitor —
to take the code and ship a competing commercial product without contributing
anything back.

Options under consideration:

- MIT / Apache-2.0 (dual) — maximally permissive, maximizes adoption and
  contribution ease, but offers no protection against commercial forks.
- GPL-3.0 — copyleft; derivative works must also be open source, but this
  does not prevent SaaS-style commercial use without releasing modifications
  (the "SaaS loophole").
- AGPL-3.0 — closes the SaaS loophole (network use counts as distribution),
  strongest protection for a project that might offer a hosted/commercial
  offering later. Some contributors and companies are wary of AGPL, which
  can slightly reduce contribution volume.
- Dual-licensing (open source license + separate commercial license) — lets
  the project owner sell commercial licenses to companies that don't want
  AGPL/GPL obligations, while the community version stays fully open. Common
  for projects with commercial ambitions, but adds legal/administrative
  overhead and requires a Contributor License Agreement (CLA) to keep the
  commercial licensing option clean.

This file will be replaced with the actual license text once a decision is
made. Until then, treat this repository as "all rights reserved" for any use
beyond reading and evaluating the code — do not build production tooling on
top of it under the assumption of a specific license.
